course: adult-life
lesson: 57
title: Age ratings in the UK, the USA, Australia and Canada, and why they exist
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Every film shown in a cinema or sold on a disc gets an age rating: a short label that says who it is suitable for. The people who rate it watch the whole film first and look at things like violence, fear, bad language and grown-up themes.
explain: Ratings exist because the same film can be fine for a 16-year-old and upsetting for an 8-year-old. Younger brains are still learning to handle strong pictures and feelings, so the rating helps families choose before they press play, not after.
explain: Vivid example: in Leeds, Charlie points at a film box in the supermarket. "What's PG, Dad?" Dad turns it over. "Parental Guidance. Anyone can watch it, but there might be a bit that scares little ones, so a grown-up should decide. See, it says 'mild threat' here."
explain: Each country has its own letters and numbers, but they all work the same way: the higher the number, the older you should be. Some are advice for parents; some are the law, and a cinema can refuse to let you in.
fact: In the UK the British Board of Film Classification (BBFC) rates films: U (Universal, for everyone), PG (Parental Guidance), 12A, 12, 15 and 18; 15 and 18 mean nobody younger may buy or see them.
fact: 12A is used only in UK cinemas: under-12s may see the film if an adult goes with them; on a disc or download the same film is rated 12.
fact: In the USA the ratings are G, PG, PG-13 (parents strongly cautioned, some material may not suit under-13s), R (under-17s need a parent or guardian with them) and NC-17 (nobody 17 or under).
fact: US television shows have their own labels, such as TV-Y for young children, TV-PG and TV-14.
fact: In Australia the ratings include G, PG, M (recommended for 15 and over, but not law) and MA15+ (under-15s must be with a parent or guardian), then R18+.
fact: In Canada the provinces rate films; ratings such as 14A mean under-14s must be with an adult.
fact: Ratings come with short words of advice, like "contains moderate violence" or "strong language", so families know why the film got its rating.
fact: Ratings are decided by trained examiners who follow published rules, not by the film makers, and not by guessing.
fact: Before a TV programme, a channel may show a warning such as "contains strong language" so viewers can choose to switch off.
fact: In the UK the watershed is 9 pm: programmes not suitable for children are not shown on ordinary TV channels before then.
fact: Streaming services use their own age labels, like 7+, 13+ or 16+, and a kids profile hides films above its age setting.
fact: Families can be stricter than a rating; parents decide what is right for their child, and different families make different choices.
fact: A U or G film can still be sad or a little scary; a rating says what is suitable, not how it will make you feel.
fact: A film can be rated again when it is re-released years later, and its rating may change.
exercise: Charlie sees "PG" on a film box. What does it mean? | Parental Guidance: a grown-up should decide | Pretty Good: the critics who saw the film liked it | Parents Go: only grown-ups may watch it | Play Gently: it is a film about sport
exercise: Freya: "Why can my little brother watch U films but not 12s?" | 12 films may have things too strong for young children | 12 films are always far longer than U films, so children get bored | U films are cheaper, so little children get those | 12 films only work on grown-ups' televisions
exercise: Grace sees "12A" on a cinema poster. What does the A mean? | Under-12s can go if an adult goes with them | The film is the first of a series of twelve films | The film is only for adults aged twelve | The film won an award for being the best film of the year
exercise: Jake sees "18" on a game his cousin is playing. What does it mean? | Nobody under 18 should buy or play it | It is the eighteenth game in a long series | You need 18 players to start the game | It takes 18 hours to finish the game
exercise: Ruby sees "PG-13" before a US film. What does it tell parents? | Some material may not suit children under 13 | Only children aged exactly 13 may watch it | The film is about a gang of thirteen-year-old children | It is the thirteenth film the studio made
exercise: Harry: "An R film in the USA means under-17s need a parent with them." Is he right? | Yes, that's what R means in the USA | No, R means it is a film made for babies only | No, R means nobody may ever watch it | Yes, but only on Saturday nights
exercise: Poppy's family in Sydney talk about "M" and "MA15+". What's the difference? | M is advice for 15+; MA15+ needs an adult with under-15s | M is for mums only; MA15+ is for mums and aunties | M is for movies; MA15+ is for music videos | M is the law; MA15+ is just a suggestion
exercise: Max sees "14A" on a poster in Toronto. What does it mean? | Under-14s must come with an adult | Only 14 people may watch at once in the cinema | The film is 14 hours long in total | The film is for adults over 14 stone
exercise: Sophie asks who decides the ratings. What's the answer? | Trained examiners at a rating board | The actors who are in the film | Whoever buys the first ticket for the film | The cinema's ice-cream seller
exercise: Theo sees "contains moderate violence" under a rating. What is it for? | It tells you why the film got its rating | It is the name of the film's sequel | It means the film is banned for adults | It is a warning that the disc is broken and won't play
exercise: Lily: "Why are there ratings at all?" What's the best answer? | To help families choose films right for a child's age | To make films more expensive for young people to watch | To stop grown-ups from watching children's films | To decide which films win prizes at the cinema each year
exercise: Oscar's mate's parents let him watch 15s; Oscar's don't. Is that fair? | Yes, each family decides what is right for its child | No, all parents must allow exactly the same | No, Oscar's parents are breaking the law | Yes, because Oscar's mate is a better reader
exercise: Evie sees "contains strong language" before a TV show. Why is it shown? | So viewers can choose whether to watch | To teach viewers some new words | Because the show is all about languages | To show that the show is very old
exercise: Finn learns about the watershed. What is it? | In the UK, adult programmes are shown only after 9 pm | A dam where TV programmes used to be filmed | The time when TV channels switch off for the night | A rule that only weather news goes before 9 pm
exercise: Ava sees "16+" on a streaming film. What is it? | The streaming service's own age label | The number of episodes in the series | The price of the film in pounds | The film's score out of twenty from critics
exercise: Leo's kids profile doesn't show some films. Why? | It hides films above its age setting | Those films have been deleted forever | The tablet's screen is too small for them | Those films only play in other countries
exercise: Noah's older cousin: "Ratings are just for babies." Put him right. | Ratings help everyone choose, and some are the law | He's right, nobody over ten needs them at all | Ratings are only for people who can't read | He's right, cinemas ignore them anyway, so why bother
exercise: Charlie learns a film's rating changed when it came out again. Can that happen? | Yes, a re-released film can be rated again | No, a rating can never change once given | Only if the actors ask for a new rating | Only if the film is made in colour again
test: Isla cries at a U film where an old dog dies. "But it's a U!" What's true? | A U film can still be sad; ratings don't measure feelings | U films are never sad, so the film must have been rated wrongly | U films are only sad for grown-ups, not children | The film must have been an 18 with the wrong label
test: Mia watches a 12 film with her parents and asks about a confusing bit. What does this show? | Watching with parents means you can ask about it | 12 films must always be watched in total silence by law | Parents are not allowed to explain 12 films | Asking questions spoils films for everyone
test: Big sister: "12A means you have to be 12 to get in, so you can't come." Put her right. | Under-12s can go with an adult | She's right, you must be at least 12 | 12A means only adults may go | 12A means you must be exactly 12
test: Alfie to his friends: "I watched a 15, it was easy, ratings are rubbish." What's wrong with his idea? | Finding it easy doesn't make it right for his age | 15 films are always too easy for teenagers to bother with | Ratings are about how hard a film is to follow | He must have watched a U by mistake
test: Dad, on the phone to Uncle Pete: "No, mate, it's after the watershed, the kids'll be in bed." When is the programme on? | After 9 pm on UK TV | Before school starts | At lunchtime on Sunday | Only in the summer
test: Mum, grumbling: "Bloody thing says 13+, but there's a bit even I didn't like." What is she noticing? | Labels help, but parents still judge for their child | Streaming labels are always exactly right for everyone | The label means she is too young to watch it | The film must have been made by a child
test: A news report: "The film has been reclassified from 18 to 15 for its anniversary release." What happened? | It was rated again and the rating changed | It was banned from all cinemas forever | It was cut down to fifteen minutes long | The same film got two different titles
test: Cousin from Melbourne: "M's illegal for under-15s here." Put her right. | M is only advice; MA15+ is the one with rules | She's right, M is the strictest rating in the whole country | M is only for films with music in them | M means nobody may watch it until midnight
test: Mum to Gran: "I'm not saying the Joneses are wrong, we just do things differently." What are they talking about? | Families can choose differently about films | The Joneses have broken a rating law | Gran has to decide for both families | Films are banned in the Joneses' house
test: Uncle in Ohio: "It's rated R, so no, buddy, not unless your mom comes too." How old is the boy, probably? | Under 17 | Over 18 | Exactly 17 | Over 21
exam: Identify the body which is principally responsible for the classification of films in the United Kingdom. | The British Board of Film Classification | The producers and directors of each film | The proprietors of individual cinemas | The audience at the first public screening
exam: Determine the meaning of the classification "12A" as applied to a cinematographic exhibition in the United Kingdom. | Persons under twelve may attend if accompanied by an adult | Persons under twelve are excluded without any exception | Attendance is restricted to persons aged exactly twelve | Attendance is restricted to adults over the age of twelve
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the "watershed" on British television. | Material unsuitable for children is shown only after 9 pm | All programmes are suspended after the hour of 9 pm | Programmes for children are shown only after 9 pm | Material of any description may be shown at any hour
exam: Ascertain the principal purpose for which age ratings are assigned to films. | To assist in selecting material suitable for a given age | To determine the price to be charged for admission | To rank films in order of their artistic merit | To prevent adults from viewing films for children
